#N canvas 535 210 1355 733 12;
#X msg 174 49 440;
#X floatatom 257 52 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 175 101 +;
#X floatatom 177 148 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X text 21 11 This patch is about demonstrating inlets. I already explored this but it is in the book and worth mentioning again.;
#X text 340 77 In the book they show the same example twice. Once with the numbers not added. The 440 in the message box needs to be clicked for the [+] object to add the number from the message box and whatever number in the number atom to be added. You can change the number in the number atom as much as you want. However \, nothing happens until you click the number in the message box. Clicking the message box sends a bang even though nothing is said about that \, although it does say it in the book. I figured that out when I made the multiple oscillators patch and routed the changing number atom to send that trigger as well to the number connected to the hot inlet on the left. That way changing either would cause the numbers to be added. Let's try that type of thing again \, to the right.;
#X floatatom 987 34 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 870 135 +;
#X floatatom 873 189 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X text 348 304 By the way \, the number box below will remain at 0 until the message box is triggered to send a bang.;
#X text 983 109 That works as I had hoped.;
#X msg 858 76 2000;
#X text 920 152 The number box sends a trigger signal to the message box and that makes the message box send out a bang. However \, you have to remember that changing a number in the message box does not make a bang be sent out from it. You have to click it or send a trigger to it.;
#X obj 150 494 ==;
#X text 129 381 This next part is also from p76 \, working with [==].;
#X msg 134 438 1;
#X msg 255 437 1;
#X msg 195 436 0;
#X msg 307 440 0;
#X text 376 460 Does an audio signal produce the output that can connect with a number box?;
#X msg 422 512 1;
#X msg 543 511 1;
#X msg 483 510 0;
#X msg 595 514 0;
#X floatatom 437 627 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 438 568 ==~;
#X text 534 581 IT DOES NOT CONNECT!;
#X text 533 617 However \, if you copy the part of the patch \, paste it somewhere and change the [==] to [==~] \, the patch cable remains connected. However \, an error message will pop up in the Pd window. The patch cable turned thick. It is something to note in case I do that by mistake.;
#X text 1 591 Remember here that in order to get a comparison to work \, you have to click on the number you want to compare on the right first or the last number clicked on the right is the one to be compared when you click on the number (message box) on the left. This is the way hot (left) and cold (right) inlets work.;
#X msg 964 504 1;
#X msg 1003 503 0;
#X msg 1057 504 1;
#X msg 1099 504 0;
#X obj 966 540 ||;
#X floatatom 964 583 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X floatatom 147 542 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X text 8 682 The default number to compare with no clicked message box on the right is the number 0;
